.draft = false,
---

A codec turns CapTP messages into bytes and back. It does not decide what a
message means or whether a reference is valid; that authority stays in the
session. Changing codecs changes the wire format, not application behavior.

glowcap ships two:

| Codec | Use it when | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) | Always, unless a measured glowcap-to-glowcap workload says otherwise | Compact and interoperable; finding an arbitrary field requires a scan |
| [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) | Both peers are glowcap and large or partially read payloads benefit from fixed offsets | Constant-time field access; larger frames and no outside interoperability |

[One message, two encodings. Syrup lays it out as variable-length byte records and Cap'n Proto as fixed 8-byte words; decode either and you get back the same message.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-codec.svg'))

# Syrup

Syrup is the OCapN format and glowcap's default. It is the only codec glowcap
uses with [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and other
implementations.

Its length-prefixed, variable-width records keep small control messages
compact. The decoder can find a frame boundary and decode it in one forward
pass. Random access is the tradeoff: reaching item *i* requires walking the
records before it.

Syrup is also glowcap's signing format. Session location signatures and
three-party handoff certificates use canonical Syrup bytes even when two
glowcap peers exchange their messages with Cap'n Proto.

# Cap'n Proto

Cap'n Proto lays messages out in 8-byte words with fixed field offsets. That
makes partial reads and random access cheap, but padding and pointer words make
small messages larger.

In glowcap it is only for two glowcap peers; Goblins does not speak it. Codec
and CapTP version are separate choices. The native preference pairs Cap'n Proto
with `glowcap-next`, while Cap'n Proto can also carry V1.0 semantics between
glowcap peers. Set `codec = .capnp` and the version you need in Zig
`SessionOptions`, or use the matching Host or binding preference.

# Choosing

Stay on Syrup for ordinary CapTP traffic. Other OCapN implementations expect
it, glowcap signs with it, and small messages stay compact.

Consider Cap'n Proto only when both peers are glowcap and your payloads are
large or field-heavy enough for fixed-offset access to matter. Measure your
actual messages: the
[performance page]($link.page('performance')) shows one small mixed-argument
call, not a universal result.

Either codec can hand the session a temporary view of a message. glowcap copies
into owned memory only when it must keep that message after decode finishes.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains how
peers negotiate the surrounding protocol profile. The
[architecture doc](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
defines the codec extension boundary for Zig consumers.
